    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "When you're moving from one activity to another like you described—the garden club list, the knitting, the travel papers—do you find it hard to focus on finishing one thing, or does your mind feel like it's racing with lots of different thoughts at the same time?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S002",
      "patient_response": "Well, it's a bit of both, I suppose. I definitely have a hard time focusing on finishing things. Like, I can start out listening at the garden club planning meeting or, you know, reading the Sunday church bulletin, but after a little while I realize I’ve missed half of what was said or printed. I just drift off. Speaking of the church bulletin, they changed the font a few weeks ago, and it's just dreadful. It's so tiny and squished together. Mildred—she's the church secretary, lovely woman but terribly stubborn—insists it saves paper, but I told her, \"Mildred, half the congregation needs a magnifying glass just to see the hymn numbers!\" My friend Betty actually brought one of those little pocket magnifiers last week, the kind with the little light built in? She got it from a catalog, I think. I used to love looking through those catalogs, all the little gadgets and things you never knew you needed. I bought a cherry pitter from one years ago, and I don't even like cherries all that much! They're so messy, and the juice stains your fingers terribly. I tried to make a cherry pie once for the church bake sale, and... oh, dear, I've lost my train of thought. Did that answer your question about my mind racing? I just can't seem to keep my attention on one thing for very long before I'm off on something else.",
