    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Have you always been this way—bouncy and easily distracted—since you were a child, or is this something that's gotten worse more recently?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Well, you know, it's funny you ask about when I was a child. I've been this way for as long as I can remember, really. Even back in grade school, my teachers used to tell my mother that I was bright but just so scattered. I was always losing my papers, talking out of turn, and just wiggling in my seat before I'd even finished my work. \n\nSpeaking of grade school, I went to this little brick schoolhouse on Elm Street. It's not there anymore, they tore it down in the late eighties to build a strip mall. Can you believe that? A strip mall where children used to learn their multiplication tables! There was a wonderful bakery in that strip mall for a while, though. They made the most divine lemon bars. My husband, God rest his soul, used to love lemon bars. He always said the secret was extra zest in the filling. We tried growing a lemon tree in the sunroom once, but it never really took off. I think it didn't get enough direct light in the winter months. Winter can be so gloomy here, don't you think? That's why my sister wants me to go to Arizona. She says the sun shines almost every day. But as I was saying earlier, the flying... oh, what were we talking about? Grade school, yes. I've always been a bit wiggly and scattered, ever since I was a little girl.",
